A||||8||Oreif||Headliner Solution!!! Look At this!||||14||
Z||000000||Oreif||08-06-2000||08:27 PM||||Today Coyote stopped by so we could do some work on our Fiero's. He showed me his new Headliner. He found a product called "Heads Up" at Auto Zone. While we were at PepBoys getting some parts, we saw the same stuff there! I bought it (Only $29.95 at both stores) Coyote was nice enough to do mine While I made and installed his Accel 8.8mm Spark Plug wires. See pic's for what was done.<BR>It came out great! They have many colors including light and dark gray. If your headliner is sagging, torn, or dirty for $29.95 and about 2 hours of work it can look like brand new!<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Headliner.jpg"> <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg"> ||||reg||14
Z||000001||Oreif||08-06-2000||11:10 PM||||It comes in Black, Light Blue, Dark Blue, Light Gray, Dark Gray, Beige, Doe Skin, Brown, Red, and Burgundy. The material has the foam backing already.||||reg||1
Z||000002||wkayl||08-06-2000||11:20 PM||||Looks like I'm going to have to stop by there.  I just removed a roof panel at a salvage yard with factory sunroof for my GT (previous owner cut in aftermarket sunroof)  I did get the headlinet, but it is lt gray.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Carrolles||08-07-2000||12:23 AM||||You can get the same foam backed headliner fabric at most fabric stores for about $7.50 per yard.  A little over a yard will give you enough to do the headliner and the sun visors.  The can of spray glue (for foam backed fabrics, I used 3M Foamfast 74) cost about $10.00 at the same fabric store and is enough to do two or three Fiero headliners.||||reg||1
Z||000004||My7Fieros||08-07-2000||02:35 AM||||I was getting ready to say the same thing Carrolles...I got all mine from a fabrics store here...$7.49 a yard!||||reg||1
Z||000005||Fformula88||08-07-2000||05:29 PM||||I re-covered my headliner with a similar material.  It turned out VERY nice.  Except I goofed it over the passenger seat.  I didn't get enough glue up there so it is sagging a little.  I haven't had the time to pull it down and fix it yet.  I did clean the backing REALLY good too.  Maybe someday if I feel ambitious...||||reg||1
Z||000006||Oreif||08-08-2000||02:15 AM||||I stopped at a fabric store on the way to work to check out the fabric. The stuff I used is thicker than the fabric store's. The Fabric store looks about the same thickness as stock. The "Heads Up" stuff is about 1/3 to 1/2 thicker than fabric store stuff. I didn't see anything at the store I stopped at that was as thick. They had it for $7.95 per yard. I prefer the thicker stuff myself.<P>Carrolles ~ I am interested in how you did the sun visors. Were you able to put the pockets in it like stock? I'm thinking of going to the Mr. Mikes leather but they don't have pockets. Can you post some pics of your sun visors?<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||filthyscarecrow||08-09-2000||03:11 AM||||okay, here's a silly question for ya. i have been toying with the idea of swapping interiors. my dead 85gt (now officially a parts car) has the grey interior and my 86 SE has the saddle. i don' tlike the saddle, adn the grey is in much better condition. the only reason i haven't done it yet is because i don't knwo how hard it is to pull that headliner off. the dashboards concern me too. anyone have any experience with this drop me an e-mail. thanky....<P>------------------<BR>me go fast.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Monkeyman||08-09-2000||11:37 AM||||It's all easy.  Just a little time consuming.  Once you remove all of the interior trim (including the 3rd brakelight [on the '86 and later] and the light pod), you just have to pull the plastic trim from around the sunroof (if you have one) and the headliner will fall on your head.  (Honestly)  The dash is easy, too.  You have to remove all of the gauge and radio pods and the speaker covers.  There is a screw under each of the speaker covers and another couple just above the dead pedals.  Remove those and slightly lift the dash as you pull it out.  There are various clips behind some of the trim pieces (such as the A pillar trim).  Be careful with these.  (You can buy new ones [recommended] if you break the old ones.)  You'll need:  (1) each--7mm, 10mm, and 13mm sockets and a T45 (I think) Torx for the seatbelt bolts.  <P>Just take your time and you can do it.  I'm one of the most mechanically challenged of the bunch here and I've successfully removed 2 interiors so far.||||reg||1
